### What is a palindrome?

A palindrome is a string that reads the same from left to right as it does from right to left. Simple examples include `"madam"`, `"level"`, and `"racecar"`. A single character is trivially a palindrome, and an empty string is also considered a palindrome because there is nothing to contradict the definition.

In the banking domain, Rajesh at National Bank of India generates reference codes for transactions. Some codes like `"ABCBA"` happen to be palindromes, and the system marks them as symmetric for a quick visual check during reconciliation.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class PalindromeBasics {
    public static boolean isPalindrome(String s) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = s.length() - 1;
        while (left < right) {
            if (s.charAt(left) != s.charAt(right)) {
                return false;
            }
            left++;
            right--;
        }
        return true;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(isPalindrome("madam"));   // true
        System.out.println(isPalindrome("bank"));    // false
        System.out.println(isPalindrome("ABCBA"));   // true
    }
}
```

```text
true
false
true
```

### How does the two-pointer technique work?

The two-pointer technique places `left` at index 0 and `right` at the last index. At each step, you compare the characters at `left` and `right`. If they differ, the string is not a palindrome and you return false immediately. If they match, you move `left` forward and `right` backward. The loop continues while `left < right`. When the pointers meet or cross, every pair has matched and the string is a palindrome.

The key insight is that a palindrome is defined by mirrored pairs. You only need to check up to the middle of the string because the second half mirrors the first half.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class TwoPointerPalindrome {
    public static boolean isPalindrome(String s) {
        char[] chars = s.toCharArray();
        int left = 0;
        int right = chars.length - 1;
        while (left < right) {
            if (chars[left] != chars[right]) {
                return false; // early exit on first mismatch
            }
            left++;
            right--;
        }
        return true;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String refCode = "SAV12321VAS";
        System.out.println(refCode + " is palindrome: " + isPalindrome(refCode));
    }
}
```

```text
SAV12321VAS is palindrome: true
```

### How is the two-pointer method different from reverse-and-compare?

The reverse-and-compare method creates a reversed copy of the string and checks if it equals the original. The two-pointer method checks characters in place without creating any new string.

| Feature | Two-pointer | Reverse-and-compare |
|---|---|---|
| Time complexity | O(n) | O(n) |
| Space complexity | O(1) | O(n) for the reversed string |
| Early exit | Yes, on first mismatch | No, must reverse the entire string first |
| Clarity | Slightly more code | Very concise with StringBuilder |

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class PalindromeComparison {
    // Method 1: Two pointers (O(1) space, early exit)
    public static boolean twoPointer(String s) {
        int left = 0, right = s.length() - 1;
        while (left < right) {
            if (s.charAt(left) != s.charAt(right)) return false;
            left++;
            right--;
        }
        return true;
    }

    // Method 2: Reverse and compare (O(n) space, no early exit)
    public static boolean reverseCompare(String s) {
        String reversed = new StringBuilder(s).reverse().toString();
        return s.equals(reversed);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String code = "ABCBA";
        System.out.println("Two-pointer: " + twoPointer(code));
        System.out.println("Reverse: " + reverseCompare(code));
    }
}
```

```text
Two-pointer: true
Reverse: true
```

### How do you handle case-insensitive palindrome checks?

Real-world palindrome checks often need to ignore case and non-alphanumeric characters. For example, `"A man, a plan, a canal: Panama"` is a palindrome when you ignore case, spaces, and punctuation. You normalise the input first, then apply the two-pointer scan.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class NormalisedPalindrome {
    public static boolean isPalindrome(String s) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = s.length() - 1;
        while (left < right) {
            while (left < right && !Character.isLetterOrDigit(s.charAt(left))) {
                left++;
            }
            while (left < right && !Character.isLetterOrDigit(s.charAt(right))) {
                right--;
            }
            if (Character.toLowerCase(s.charAt(left)) != Character.toLowerCase(s.charAt(right))) {
                return false;
            }
            left++;
            right--;
        }
        return true;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(isPalindrome("A man, a plan, a canal: Panama")); // true
        System.out.println(isPalindrome("Rajesh")); // false
    }
}
```

```text
true
false
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases in palindrome checking include empty strings, single characters, strings with all identical characters, and strings with special characters.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class PalindromeEdgeCases {
    public static boolean isPalindrome(String s) {
        if (s == null) return false;
        int left = 0, right = s.length() - 1;
        while (left < right) {
            if (s.charAt(left) != s.charAt(right)) return false;
            left++;
            right--;
        }
        return true;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Empty: " + isPalindrome(""));        // true
        System.out.println("Single: " + isPalindrome("A"));      // true
        System.out.println("All same: " + isPalindrome("aaaa")); // true
        System.out.println("Two diff: " + isPalindrome("ab"));   // false
        System.out.println("Null: " + isPalindrome(null));       // false
    }
}
```

```text
Empty: true
Single: true
All same: true
Two diff: false
Null: false
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

The two-pointer palindrome check runs in O(n) time, where n is the length of the string, because each character is visited at most once. The space complexity is O(1) because only two integer index variables are used.

If normalisation is done by creating a cleaned string, the space becomes O(n) for the cleaned copy. The in-place normalisation approach shown above with the skip-non-alphanumeric inner loops avoids this extra allocation and stays at O(1) space.

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is using `==` to compare characters from a `String` obtained via `charAt()`. For primitive `char` values, `==` works correctly. The real bug is forgetting to handle case sensitivity or forgetting the null check.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class PalindromeMistakes {
    // WRONG: does not handle case
    public static boolean wrongPalindrome(String s) {
        int left = 0, right = s.length() - 1;
        while (left < right) {
            if (s.charAt(left) != s.charAt(right)) return false;
            left++;
            right--;
        }
        return true;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // "Madam" should be a palindrome ignoring case, but this returns false
        System.out.println("Wrong: " + wrongPalindrome("Madam"));

        // Fix by lowercasing
        System.out.println("Correct: " + wrongPalindrome("Madam".toLowerCase()));
    }
}
```

```text
Wrong: false
Correct: true
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

Interviewers often extend the basic palindrome check with harder variants:

- **Valid Palindrome II**: can you make the string a palindrome by removing at most one character?
- **Longest Palindromic Substring**: find the longest contiguous palindrome within a string.
- **Palindrome Partitioning**: split the string into parts where every part is a palindrome.
- **Number Palindrome**: check if a number is a palindrome without converting it to a string.

Each of these builds on the two-pointer foundation. Understanding the basic technique thoroughly is the key to solving the variants.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class PalindromeFollowUp {
    // Valid Palindrome II: can remove at most one character
    public static boolean validPalindromeII(String s) {
        int left = 0, right = s.length() - 1;
        while (left < right) {
            if (s.charAt(left) != s.charAt(right)) {
                return isSubPalindrome(s, left + 1, right)
                    || isSubPalindrome(s, left, right - 1);
            }
            left++;
            right--;
        }
        return true;
    }

    private static boolean isSubPalindrome(String s, int lo, int hi) {
        while (lo < hi) {
            if (s.charAt(lo) != s.charAt(hi)) return false;
            lo++;
            hi--;
        }
        return true;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(validPalindromeII("abca")); // true, remove 'c' or 'b'
        System.out.println(validPalindromeII("abc"));  // false
    }
}
```

```text
true
false
```
